                      (../../defs/coco.d):00194         
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00195         ****************************************
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00196         * Dynamic Address Translator Definitions
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00197         *
     0008             (../../defs/coco.d):00198         DAT.BlCt            EQU       8                   D.A.T. blocks/address space
     2000             (../../defs/coco.d):00199         DAT.BlSz            EQU       (256/DAT.BlCt)*256  D.A.T. block size
     0010             (../../defs/coco.d):00200         DAT.ImSz            EQU       DAT.BlCt*2          D.A.T. Image size
     FFE0             (../../defs/coco.d):00201         DAT.Addr            EQU       -(DAT.BlSz/256)     D.A.T. MSB Address bits
     FF91             (../../defs/coco.d):00202         DAT.Task            EQU       $FF91               Task Register address
     0020             (../../defs/coco.d):00203         DAT.TkCt            EQU       32                  Number of DAT Tasks
     FFA0             (../../defs/coco.d):00204         DAT.Regs            EQU       $FFA0               DAT Block Registers base address
     333E             (../../defs/coco.d):00205         DAT.Free            EQU       $333E               Free Block Number
     003F             (../../defs/coco.d):00206         DAT.BlMx            EQU       $3F                 Maximum Block number
     0040             (../../defs/coco.d):00207         DAT.BMSz            EQU       $40                 Memory Block Map size
     0000             (../../defs/coco.d):00208         DAT.WrPr            EQU       0                   no write protect
     0000             (../../defs/coco.d):00209         DAT.WrEn            EQU       0                   no write enable
     0000             (../../defs/coco.d):00210         SysTask             EQU       0                   Coco System Task number
     003F             (../../defs/coco.d):00211         IOBlock             EQU       $3F
     003F             (../../defs/coco.d):00212         ROMBlock            EQU       $3F
     007F             (../../defs/coco.d):00213         IOAddr              EQU       $7F
     0001             (../../defs/coco.d):00214         ROMCount            EQU       1                   number of blocks of ROM (High RAM Block)
     0001             (../../defs/coco.d):00215         RAMCount            EQU       1                   initial blocks of RAM
     0005             (../../defs/coco.d):00216         MoveBlks            EQU       DAT.BlCt-ROMCount-2 Block numbers used for copies
     0001             (../../defs/coco.d):00217         BlockTyp            EQU       1                   chk only first bytes of RAM block
     0002             (../../defs/coco.d):00218         ByteType            EQU       2                   chk entire block of RAM
     0001             (../../defs/coco.d):00219         Limited             EQU       1                   chk only upper memory for ROM modules
     0002             (../../defs/coco.d):00220         UnLimitd            EQU       2                   chk all NotRAM for modules
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00221         * NOTE: this check assumes any NotRAM with a module will
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00222         *       always start with $87CD in first two bytes of block
     0001             (../../defs/coco.d):00223         RAMCheck            EQU       BlockTyp            chk only beg bytes of block
     0001             (../../defs/coco.d):00224         ROMCheck            EQU       Limited             chk only upper few blocks for ROM
     003F             (../../defs/coco.d):00225         LastRAM             EQU       IOBlock             maximum RAM block number
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00226         
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00227         ***************************
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00228         * Color Computer 3 Specific
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00229         *
     0001             (../../defs/coco.d):00230         MappedIO            EQU       true                (Actually False but it works better this way)
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00231         
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00232         ********************
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00233         * Hardware addresses
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00234         *
     FF00             (../../defs/coco.d):00235         GIMERegs            EQU       $FF00               Base address of GIME registers
     FF92             (../../defs/coco.d):00236         IrqEnR              EQU       $FF92               GIME IRQ enable/status register
     FF9A             (../../defs/coco.d):00237         BordReg             EQU       $FF9A               Border color register
     FFB0             (../../defs/coco.d):00238         PalAdr              EQU       $FFB0               Palette registers
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00239         
     0007             (../../defs/coco.d):00240         HW.Page             SET       $07                 Device descriptor hardware page
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00241         
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00242         * KrnBlk defines the block number of the 8K RAM block that is mapped to
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00243         * the top of CPU address space ($E000-$FFFF) for the system process, and
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00244         * which holds the Kernel. The top 2 pages of this CPU address space ($FFE0-
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00245         * $FFFF) has two special properties. Firstly, it contains the I/O space.
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00246         * Secondly, the parts that contain RAM map are not affected by the DAT
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00247         * mappings but, instead, *always* map that RAM to a fixed RAM block
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00248         * (KrnBlk). When a user process is mapped in, and requests enough memory,
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00249         * it will end up with its own block assigned for CPU address space $E000-
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00250         * $FFFF but the top of the address space is unusable by the user process:
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00251         * it still contains the I/O and the bit of RAM that's mapped to KrnBlk.
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00252         * Usually, the value of KrnBlk is fixed for a particular hardware design;
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00253         * For the COCO GIME, it's always $3F.
     003F             (../../defs/coco.d):00254         KrnBlk              SET       $3F
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00255         
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00256                             ENDC
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00257                             ENDC
                      (../../defs/coco.d):00258         
